== Requirements (simple)
- project structure (broger)
  + Find a recent issue that educated people believe is still an open question
    - What is the open ended question
      - Is online age verification actually as effective in protecting children using the internet as the government makes it seem?
    - explain in detail - context
      - Australian government implemented age verification to "protect kids from harmful content on online platforms"
        - Add extra details such as fines and quotes from e-safety website on the rules
        - timeline of events. (when did this idea come to be, when was it passed, etc)
        - quotes from e-safety commissioner regarding reasons and justification (pr)
        - how does age verification work. look into all 3 different methods
        - public backlash summary
        - quotes from reddit or something (need third party accounts of how bad it is)
          - possibly look into how different platforms reacted to it. (i.e. reddit was more angry than tiktok)
        - question gets raised. Does age verification actually work (is it protecting kids)
          - define work
        - another question: are people over the age of 18 collateral damage?
        - the future of the bill (new duty of care proposal)
    - Why is it uncertain
      - new technology and policies.
      - government figures show that this amount of people have been banned on day one
      - government figures show that this amount of people are still wrongfully on the platforms today (graph population)
      - what are the governments sources for this. are they credible or "wishful thinking"
      - are online platforms going to be transparent about these figures when legislation requires them to follow a rule.
      - What makes social media companies inclinded to disobey. (what is their business model, different countries. i.e. tiktok -> china, meta -> usa)
    - What is at stake
      - what happens when age verification goes wrong (both adults being children and vice versa)
      - evidence of it going wrong (anywhere credible)
      - Who is affected. Almost all adults will have to verify if they are kids. even if the average demographic of a platform is mostly adults (facebook, nsfw websites)
      - will the bill fall out of fasion and become obselete in the coming years
      - write about recent american execution and how e-safety commissioner was unable to stop it

  +  "what would clifford do"
    - What would clifford's advice dictate in this situation and why
    - What would clifford's advice acheive.
    - Why?

  + delve into clifford's advice
    - Is it reasonable
    - Why?

  + conclusion
    - critically evaluate clifford's position
    - incoorporate information in section 3

